6.3.1    Treasury address can be set to zero
     File(s): src/validator_registry/validator_registry.cairo
     Description: The Treasury address is a contract or account address to which fees are sent. However, this address can be set to zero.
     If this address is set to zero, fees cannot be transferred to that address because the ERC20::transfer(...) function will revert. This
     breaks related delegator functions.

 1   fn set_settings(ref self: ContractState, settings: ValidatorRegistrySettings) {
 2       // @audit-issue Treasury address can be set to zero
 3       self.common.assert_config_manager();
 4

 5       self.settings.write(settings);
 6   }

 1   fn transfer_fee_to_treasury(
 2       ref self: ContractState, token: ContractAddress, rewards: u128,
 3   ) -> u128 {
 4

 5       if (fees > 0) {
 6           let treasury = settings.treasury;
 7           erc20helper::transfer(STRK_TOKEN_ADDRESS, treasury, fees.into());
 8           // @audit-issue This call will revert if treasury address is zero.
 9           self
10                .emit(
11                    Fee { amount: fees.into(), token: STRK_TOKEN_ADDRESS, receiver: treasury },
12                );
13       }
14   }

     Recommendation(s): Consider checking the address is not zero before updating storage.
     Status: Fixed
     Update from the client: Fixed in e1c0028

